<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tepehuán Revolt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1616">1616</a>–<a href="/years/1620">1620</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tepehuán Revolt was a rising of the Tepehuán people of Nueva <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biscay">Vizcaya</a>, in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, against Spanish rule that broke out in November 1616. Led by a prophet who promised to drive out the Spaniards, the rebels killed settlers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> and Franciscan missionaries and destroyed missions and mining settlements around Durango. Spanish forces had suppressed it by about 1620, leaving the region badly depopulated.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tepehuan-revolt.jpg" alt="Tepehuán Revolt" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur; derivative work by Smallchief · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tepuhuan_location_1616.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
